  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "EFF lays out its policy position that ALPR mass surveillance is irredeemably harmful and should be eliminated, while urging courts and legislatures to impose warrant requirements, deletion deadlines, and use restrictions in the meantime. The post describes EFF's multi-front strategy: opposing local ALPR purchases, lobbying state legislatures, litigating (including a suit against San Jose PD's warrantless searches and a suit to block sharing with federal/out-of-state agencies), and investigative reporting.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "EFF's position is that ALPR mass surveillance\u2014the indiscriminate, continuous collection and retention of location data on every driver, regardless of suspicion\u2014should not exist.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "There is no configuration of an ALPR network that eliminates this risk, because the risk is the mass surveillance itself, not a misuse of it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "EFF and the ACLU of Northern California are suing on behalf of two community groups, SIREN and CAIR-CA, arguing that the San Jose Police Department's practice of letting officers search stored plate data\u2014to the tune of over 100,000 times a year\u2014without a warrant violates the California Constitution.",
      "paragraph_idx": 15
    }
  ],
